# Which layer is composed from which, at layer grain, for the parts that cross a document. One row
# per foreign part: which composed layer, and which layer of which other filing it is composed from.
#
# The widest `demoted`, promoted back to a reference
#   A `callActivity` names a process, so edges between layers collapse to pairs of documents, and
#   the layer survives only inside `@name` as `labour <- merge-us-member/labour`: readable by a
#   person, unresolvable by a tool. `tRelationship` carries `source` and `target` as QNames,
#   `minOccurs 1 maxOccurs unbounded`, plus a required `type` string.
#
# Why a relationship and not an `association`
#   The `type` attribute is the whole reason. An association has no name and no type, so its only
#   distinguishing marks are its ends, and a second use makes two facts indistinguishable. That is
#   real, not feared: `merge-group-composition` and `merge-holding-composition` would each carry
#   coupling lines and part lines side by side, in the two documents where "a coupling is never
#   evidence for a fusion" matters most. A relationship says which relation it is, in a required
#   field.
#
# The `extensionElements` refusal does not reach this
#   That refusal is: ForeignId crosses by naming a position in the other document and never by
#   importing any of it, and an extension element is importing all of it. A relationship carries two
#   QNames and a type string: it names positions and imports nothing. The refusal is about a content
#   model, not about a chapter of the standard.
#
# Foreign parts only
#   A local part is a `childLaneSet` and already keyed `(filing, layer)`, so it never loses the
#   grain and owes no repair.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part crossing a document; BPMN 2.0 tRelationship source/target.
SELECT c.composition, c.composed_layer, c.part_filing, c.part_layer, c.part_notation
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc)
) c
WHERE NOT c.is_local
